ack has no immediate VCF (Victory by Continuous Four) sequence that can outrace White\'s threat.\n\n**2. Strategy:**\n*   Black **must** block White\'s Open 3 immediately. The valid blocking points are **N7** and **J11**.\n*   **Comparing N7 vs J11:**\n    *   If Black blocks at **N7**: White might try to extend the line at J11 (forming a 4). Black would then be forced to block at I12. I12 is a central board position with reasonable utility. Also, N7 connects loosely to Black\'s stone at L7.\n    *   If Black blocks at **J11**: White might extend at N7 (forming a 4). Black would be forced to block at O6. O6 is on the edge of the board and holds very little strategic value.\n*   Therefore, blocking at N7 is the positional superior choice while satisfying the urgent defensive requirement.\n\n**Evaluation:**\nWhite poses an immediate lethal threat with an Open 3 diagonal at K10-L9-M8. Black has no immediate winning combination to ignore this threat and must block at either N7 or J11. Blocking at N7 is strategically better as it keeps the game in the center if White persists, whereas blocking at J11 could force Black to play on the edge (O6).\n\n**Reason:**\nTo prevent White from creating an Open 4 (and winning) on the K10-L9-M8 diagonal. N7 is chosen over J11 because it forces a better defensive follow-up shape (I12 vs O6) and stays connected to Black\'s existing stones.\n\nAnswer:\n{"col": "N", "row": 7}'}
